WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:06.380
Lasers are everywhere. In doctors' surgeries,
your DVD player, even in the club.

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:13.560
But before lasers came MASERs - like lasers 
but using microwaves instead of light.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:18.320
Based on an effect predicted by Einstein,
MASERs were invented in the 1950s.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.780
But scientists only ever 
managed to make them

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:24.700
either at super-cold temperatures
or a high vacuum.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:29.200
They did find some uses, like amplifying
faint signals from deep space.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.160
But they were soon eclipsed
 by the much more practical laser.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:37.460
Well now scientists have found a new way
to generate MASERs

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:38.900
at room temperature.

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:42.120
And they did it with
diamonds.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:46.240
I went to the London Centre for
 Nanotechnology to find out more.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:49.340
I'm Jonathan Breeze,

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:51.560
I'm a research fellow at
Imperial College London.

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:55.460
and I've been working on room-temperature 
MASERs for the past eight years.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:59.960
And recently we managed to demonstrate 
a continuous room temperature MASER

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:01.680
that uses Diamond.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.460
Before getting to that though, Jon took
me through how MASERs and lasers work.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:10.660
the MASER acronym and
the laser acronym both stand for

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:15.800
either Microwave or Light, Amplification 
by Stimulated Emission of Radiation.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:19.940
In order to get stimulated emission
what you need is a population

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:24.689
of electrons where you have two energy
levels, and there are more electrons in a

00:01:24.689 --> 00:01:27.440
higher energy level than 
a lower energy level.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:33.040
This 'putting electrons into a higher state'
is called a population inversion.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:36.700
If you can make one of those 
then the MASER fun can begin.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:41.700
An incoming photon causes one of the electrons 
to drop down to a lower energy level.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.140
When it does that it emits a photon,

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:49.140
and not just any photon, one that's identical
to the photon that just zoomed by.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:53.360
These then trigger more electrons 
to drop releasing more photons

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.580
in a chain reaction that scientists call
stimulated emission.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.840
It's a sort of  exponential 
photocopier for photons.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:07.920
and if the photons are in the microwave part 
of the spectrum, boom - you get a MASER.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:14.260
So it was possible to make the population inversion
in rubies, which they used for MASERs.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:21.360
But the population didn't last very long so it 
only lasted for a fraction of a billionth of a second.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:24.000
And so they had to cool it 
down to cryogenic temperatures

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.420
to make it last for a few 
millionths of a second.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.700
So it was still quite short amount of time.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:32.300
Jon and his colleagues
needed to find a material

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.520
that could sustain 
a population inversion

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:36.160
at room temperatures.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:39.140
They had some success with an organic polymer

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:41.660
but that could only generate 
a pulsed MASER.

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:45.460
To make a continuous version
they turned to diamonds.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:49.380
So you can see actually, this is the core of the MASER.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:53.480
And then inside you can 
see the diamond NV centre,

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:57.060
NV centre diamond. There are many
colour centers in diamonds and

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:01.980
NV center is one of the most extensively
researched colour centre in the diamond.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:06.880
NV centers, or nitrogen vacancies, are
defects in the diamond structure

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:08.740
which also make it pink.

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:12.260
Where carbon atoms are knocked out,
 electrons sit in the gaps.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:16.420
The electrons can exist at levels that
are separated by the right amount of

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:18.300
energy to emit microwaves.

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:20.900
And the diamond's 
stable structure keeps a

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:24.120
population inversion
even at room temperature.

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:27.560
As well as the diamond 
there's a sapphire resonator

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:31.940
which acts like a mirror to help intensify
the microwaves the MASER produces.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:36.140
And everything is kept
in a copper housing called a cavity.

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:42.580
So basically this MASER
assembly comprising the cavity

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:46.160
the sapphire ring resonator 
and the MASER medium - this diamond

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:50.880
will go in between the poles of the magnet, 
a powerful magnet.

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:56.100
And then what we do, actually 
we shine a green laser.

00:03:56.100 --> 00:04:00.680
And when the laser hits  the NV centres, 
the diamond glows a rich pink.

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:05.140
that's actually just a by-product
of a much more important process

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:08.120
putting the electrons into their
higher energy state.

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:11.980
When this is combined with a
precisely calibrated magnetic field

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:14.780
you get that crucial population inversion.

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:18.240
Then add the sapphire resonator
and the copper housing,

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.060
and out pops a MASER.

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:24.060
When we first got involved with the diamond
I did quite a bit theory.

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:26.080
I'm generally theory led,

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:30.260
so I don't want to waste my time doing an
experiment that I know won't work.

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:33.240
And all the theory pointed 
towards it working.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:40.340
We had been trying for about a year to kind of
observe this amazing phenomenon

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:44.220
and we'd had no kind of
positive results.

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:47.060
And then we saw this 
quirky phenomenon

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:52.800
and I knew what that was and I got very excited. 
So the next day everyone was there,

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:56.860
We all excited actually
so it was a really great day.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:59.280
You guessed it,
they'd made a MASER.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:01.600
I had to go for a walk 
to calm myself down.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:07.320
So yeah, it wasn't a 'Eureka moment' but it 
was definitely, you know, a 'we did it moment.'

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:12.000
Their MASER is still pretty weak,
but it's a proof of principle.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.360
And the fact that it uses 
NV center diamonds is exciting,

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:19.640
because these are already being 
studied for other applications.

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:24.200
Quantum computing, at room temperature, 
and also what's called quantum metrology.

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:28.200
The fact that we we use that kind of
 diamond for our MASER,

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:32.900
should hopefully open up a new branch in this 
kind of quantum diamond technology.

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:37.000
This technique could not only make for 
cheaper and more convenient MASERs,

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:41.500
it might mean MASERs end up being
used in loads more places than today.

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:46.060
But since this device is new it's hard
 to know yet where that will be.

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:50.580
Over the past half century or so 
lasers have cropped up everywhere.

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:55.280
Now the MASER's found a new lease of life
who knows where it could end up.

